Hello,

I have some questions regarding the use of hcov arguments (h2 and h3) to fit hybrid mixture models in SECR.
I used them to model individual heterogeneity (sex and age classes).

A] I do not understand what transformation of the parameter estimates pmix is done when using h3?

Estimates with h2:
The two transformed pmix add up to 1.
pmix.h22            -0.8211026
pmix  logit           0.3055297  and invlogit( -0.8211026) = 0.3055297
pmix  logit           0.6944703

Estimates with h3:
The three transformed pmix also add up to 1, although it is quite strange that two are always identical.
pmix.h32            0.6209174           invlogit(0.6209174) = 0.6504272
pmix.h33           -0.3358121           invlogit(-0.3358121) = 0.4168271
pmix  logit           0.4116048
pmix  logit           0.2941976
pmix  logit           0.2941976

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

I also fitted regression splines to model the spatial trend (terrain roughness ~ density).

My best model, according to AIC, is:
(g0 ~ 1, D ~ s(ruggedness, k = 6, fx = TRUE), sigma ~ h3 + session)

Models with high values of "k" were experimental as I do not have much data. I was surprised to see the model with k=6 coming out as the best one. However, I did not get standard errors for all estimated parameters. I thought the model might be too complicated for the amount of data I feed, and it might not converge properly. Models with lower k values also had good AIC weights (slightly lower values) but the number of NAs for standard errors reduced.
With k=4 and h2 (instead of h3), all standard errors were calculated.

Is it correct to assume that -- when standard errors are not calculated (or when they take abnormally high values) -- the models do not converge very well and therefore they should not be included?

Thanks very much
Kind regards,

Elsa

